cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  Public domain (CC0). Citing is a courtesy to your readers, not to us — pin the
  version tag and the commit, because main moves daily. Cite it as what it is:
  model legislation, a research draft, never enacted law. Cite a provision by
  section and link by line: model_act_v3_4.txt#L236 opens SEC. 4; the section-to-line
  index is in the README. The archived record carries a DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.22029795. Every authority the draft relies on is listed, with the
  proposition each is cited for, in standards/table_of_authorities.md.
title: "Model Act — Frontier AI Public Welfare Offenses"
abstract: >-
  A public-domain model state criminal statute applying the responsible corporate
  officer doctrine — United States v. Dotterweich, 320 U.S. 277 (1943), and United
  States v. Park, 421 U.S. 658 (1975) — to frontier artificial intelligence. It
  assigns personal, non-delegable duties to natural persons with final material
  authority and practical power over the relevant risk. A covered frontier model
  exceeds a bracketed 10^26-operation threshold or is prospectively designated as
  frontier-equivalent; specified records duties begin at a lower threshold. The base
  individual offense requires failure to exercise due care and does not require
  actual knowledge; knowing or wilful conduct changes the tier. Separate entity
  liability neither substitutes for personal liability nor permits a company to
  insure, indemnify, reimburse, or otherwise absorb an individual’s penalty. The
  project includes fourteen statutory sections, drafting notes, draft regulations,
  a public audit record, and an append-only errata register. Drafted with AI
  assistance disclosed. Research draft; never enacted; no completed review claimed.
